    "final_verdict": "VERDICT: CHANGES REQUESTED\n\nThe `primaryIdentifier(Owner)` indirection is well done \u2014 it's exactly the single source of truth the spec asks for, so the \"customerCode \u2192 memberId\" migration becomes a one-method change. Keep it. The problem is how the JSON is produced.\n\n**Hand-rolled JSON serialization is the wrong unit, and non-idiomatic for this codebase.** `ownerCreatedEvent` builds JSON via string concatenation and leans on a bespoke `jsonString` char-by-char escaper. This is a Spring Boot app and Jackson is a first-class dependency here (direct `tools.jackson.core` deps in `pom.xml`, and `@Json*` annotations already used across `model/` \u2014 `User`, `Vet`, `Role`, `BaseEntity`, etc.). Serialization is a solved, owned concern in this project; reimplementing it inline is the kind of thing a maintainer would not expect to find living in a business service.\n\nConcretely:\n\n- **Model the event as a type.** The spec calls for an *immutable structured* event, but it's emitted as a concatenated `String` \u2014 neither structured nor immutable as a type. Define a small `record OwnerCreatedEvent(long seq, Integer ownerId, String customerCode, Integer membershipLevel, String event)` (or similar) and serialize it with an `ObjectMapper`. That makes the shape self-documenting and the escaping/formatting Jackson's job.\n\n- **`jsonString` doesn't belong in `ClinicServiceImpl`.** It's a general-purpose JSON-escaping utility with zero clinic semantics, planted in an already-large service class \u2014 it nudges the class further toward a catch-all. With Jackson doing serialization this helper disappears entirely.\n\n- **Minor, falls out of the above:** the field assembly is internally inconsistent \u2014 `customerCode` is escaped via `jsonString` while `membershipLevel` and `ownerId` are concatenated raw. It's safe today (both are `Integer`), but it's exactly the manual-correctness burden that delegating to Jackson removes.\n\nThe `OWNER_CREATED_SEQ` counter and the single `AUDIT.info(...)` emission point alongside the human-readable line are fine structurally; the change needed is to route the event object through the serializer the codebase already uses rather than building the string by hand.",
